WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.940
U.S. and China extend trade truths to January, giving supply chains, temporary relief from tariff uncertainty. This is Omni View, episode 186. U.S. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent announced that Washington and Beijing have agreed to keep existing tariff and export arrangements in place for another two months. The extension follows talks between Bessent and Chinese Vice Premier Ha Liefeng. Chinese President Xi Jinping began a state visit to Washington the same week.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:33.580
Besson described the discussions as very productive.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:40.720
Nikai Asia reports the move as a pragmatic pause that lets companies plan shipments without immediate new duties.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:48.260
Al Jazeera frames the same talks as part of wider U.S. pressure on Beijing over technology and security issues.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:55.160
Both outlets note the two-month window ends in January, after which either side could reimpose measures.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:00.860
The announcement came as she opened his first state visit to Washington in over a decade.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:06.300
The empirical ground both sides accept is that the lists of disputed tariff items and

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.960
export licenses stayed frozen during the talks.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.680
The divide appears in how the pause is described, with one account stressing business planning

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:18.160
time and the other highlighting ongoing security tensions.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:23.880
Supporters of the pragmatic view can see that any mismatch in customs codes could reopen

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:27.080
disputes quickly once the January deadline arrives.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:32.500
What happens next is a new round of technical talks between Treasury and Commerce teams

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:35.680
to reconcile data before the extension expires.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:41.920
Nepal's government is trying to raise roughly $5 billion after recent floods caused widespread

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:42.600
damage.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:47.800
Prime Minister Balendra Shaw described the floods as a warning about climate change.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:52.360
International aid pledges have so far fallen short of the amount requested.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:55.900
Shaw made the appeal during the UN General Assembly.

00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:01.400
Nikae Asia notes that global donors are pulling back on large climate-related grants.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:07.120
Al Jazeera reports Shaw's appeal at the UN General Assembly for faster action on emissions.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:14.600
The two accounts agree on the funding gap and differ mainly on whether the shortfall reflects donor fatigue or shifting priorities.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:18.780
Shaw labeled the event a warning to the world on climate action.

